Introduction
A trading signal that looks strong can still just be a repackaged bet on volatility or trend strength. A momentum reading grows mechanically with bigger bars, and "the market has been going up for two weeks" shows up in a plain moving-average slope for free. Feature neutralization is the check that tells the two apart: regress the raw signal on a small set of confound factors over a rolling window, and trade the residual rather than the raw value. The residual is, by construction, linearly uncorrelated with the confounds
inside the window it was fitted on
— what that does and does not establish about the trading sequence that follows is the subject of this article, not a detail left for the end.
This article implements the mechanism as a native MQL5 library: a rolling-window ordinary-least-squares engine with its own Gauss-Jordan solver, used by an Expert Advisor that can trade either the raw signal or the neutralized residual (a one-input switch, so the same compiled EA produces both backtests under identical settings), an indicator that plots both series, and a Python companion script that validates the solver, stress-tests it, and replays the backtest on data read either from a bundled CSV or directly from a MetaTrader 5 terminal.
The project is five files: NeutralizerEngine.mqh (the engine), FeatureNeutralizer_EA.mq5 (the Expert Advisor), FeatureNeutralizer_Ind.mq5 (the chart indicator), feature_neutralizer_companion.py (offline validation and figures), and XAUUSD_M5_2025-01-07_to_2025-01-10.csv (a fixed real-data snapshot, not required once a terminal is available — see the data-source section below). All five ship in one archive, MQL5.zip ; the Files table at the end of this article lists the path each one unpacks to.
Why a "good-looking" signal can be a mirage
A 20-bar rate-of-change momentum signal on XAUUSD M5 is mechanically larger in magnitude when the underlying is more volatile, because bigger bars produce bigger price differences over any fixed lookback — a backtest that looks strong in volatile, trending stretches and flat in quiet ones may just be tracking ATR. The same happens with trend strength: a positive momentum reading during a strong trend is partly genuine continuation and partly "the market has been going up," which a plain ADX reading already captures.
Neutralization does not add information; it removes a specific, named kind of contamination. Regressing the raw signal on a volatility proxy and a trend-slope proxy over a rolling window and keeping only the residual means: whatever those two factors could have explained about the signal, this residual does not contain. That is a narrower and more checkable claim than "found new alpha," and the rest of this article is deliberately built around that narrower claim rather than a broader one the code cannot support.
The math: rolling regression and the standardized correlation system
For a rolling window of N bars, let y be the raw signal and F the k confound factors. Centering both and dividing each factor by its own standard deviation over the window gives standardized factors Z; the fitted intercept and slopes solve the factor correlation system:
(ZᵀZ/N) g = (Zᵀyc/N)
b_k = g_k / sd(F_k)
b_0 = mean(y) - sum_k b_k * mean(F_k)
Z
T
Z/N is the factor correlation matrix (unit diagonal), so it is already on a fixed, known scale before any pivoting happens — that is the point of standardizing first: a pivot tolerance applied to a correlation matrix means the same thing (a share of variance inflation) regardless of whether the raw factors are priced in 0.0001 units or 100,000 units. The regression coefficients are converted back to the original factor units afterward (b_k = g_k / sd(F_k)); the residual and R² are then computed from those original-unit coefficients, so neither depends on the standardization being an internal implementation detail versus something the caller has to worry about.
The residual applied to a bar that is
not
in the fitted window is the neutralized signal: residual = y − (b₀ + b₁f₁ + ... + b_k f_k) . The property that this residual is uncorrelated with the factors is an
in-sample
one — it holds, by the normal equations, for the exact window the beta was fitted on. Applying that same beta to a bar outside the window (which is what every live and backtested trade does) does not inherit the guarantee automatically; it only holds to the extent the factor relationship is stable from one window to the next. The "Numerical correctness validation" section below checks the in-sample property directly and reports it as exactly that, and the out-of-sample decorrelation reported in the Testing section is an empirical result on this dataset, not a mathematical guarantee.
NeutralizerEngine.mqh: the rolling-window engine
CFeatureNeutralizer holds the rolling window in a circular buffer and exposes exactly the four operations an EA or indicator needs: Init() , AddObservation() , GetResidual() , and the diagnostics. FN_NUM_FACTORS fixes the factor count at compile time and sizes every array in the class; it is checked again at runtime against an EA input so a stale #define fails loudly instead of silently mis-sizing an array.
