Two numerical-stability points worth being explicit about, since both are real weaknesses of the normal-equations approach and are not fixed by pivoting alone: solving through (Z
T
Z)β=Z
T
y squares the condition number of the design relative to solving the original least-squares problem directly (e.g. via QR or SVD), so two factors that are only moderately correlated in the raw data become more strongly correlated in the squared system, and partial pivoting controls
which
row is used as the pivot, not the underlying conditioning of the problem being solved. Standardizing the factors before forming the correlation matrix removes the scale-dependence objection (the matrix is a correlation matrix either way, with unit diagonal, so a fixed pivot tolerance means the same thing on XAUUSD as on a pair priced three orders of magnitude smaller) but it does not remove the squared-condition-number cost itself.
For the 2-factor case used throughout this article the squaring is a second-order concern; a reader extending this to many, more-correlated factors should expect the normal-equations route to lose more precision than a QR-based solver would, and should verify that with the stress test in this article's companion script before trusting it on their own factor set.
